{{thought {version #1}}
 {title Greed is Neutral}
 {uuid 2f9283c0-0c71-42fa-966d-e19ee6afaead}
 {published #"2026/09/27 23:21 +0100"}
 {description
  A common narrative on the left is that greed or often {q corporate greed} is the cause of many issues of society.
  This isn't true and it is a harmful oversimplification.
  A CEO has no choice about maximising profits\; they don't do it because they're greedy but because they have no other choice.
  Even ignoring the fact that it is a legal obligation, a company which fails to maximise profits will be outcompeted by one that does and thus the whole enterprise is ruined and everyone loses their jobs.
  It isn't always a bad thing.
  Sometimes people need to be fired, sometimes artists need to be told no.
  Reducing profit maximisation to the result of individual greed is hiding the actual systemic issues and misaligned incentives that drive profit maximisation and decide whether it is harmful or helpful.
  Take global warming, often decried as the result of a few greedy corporations evilly burning fossil fuels.
  Replacing all the greedy fossil fuel CEOs with nicer people would not resolve the issue.
  A carbon tax on the other hand would do much better, as companies in their greed would reduce their emissions of carbon in response to the increased epxense of doing so.
  Indeed the root cause of the problem is that previously they were able to emit carbon and let others bear the cost of the warming planet.
  Just as the credo {q greed is good} is a gross misunderstanding of the world, {q greed is bad} is also reductive.
  Greed is a tool that can be used for different ends, it will exist in any economic system and the best ones will make use of it instead of denying it.}}
